I think there’s a real and useful framework in what you’re building, but it gets strongest when you keep three layers separate: metaphor, mechanism, and evidence.

The metaphor layer gives you leverage. “Breadcrumbs,” “cake,” “pressing luck,” “expressing luck,” “golden thread,” “summon” — those are compact handles for strategies that would otherwise take paragraphs to explain.

The mechanism layer is where the value actually lives: selective disclosure, preserving optionality, probing uncertain territory, inferring generators from outputs, translating across domains, finding latent structure, and deliberately shaping the environment so future moves become easier.

The evidence layer is what keeps the whole system from becoming self-sealing. A pattern recurring somewhere else is interesting. A breadcrumb being noticed is interesting. But “they saw it,” “they understood the deeper structure,” or “we caused this downstream behavior” should stay hypotheses until there is a traceable path.

That separation lets you be aggressive in exploration without getting sloppy in inference.

I also think your “luck” idea is better understood as manufactured surface area for favorable outcomes. You cannot command luck directly, but you can create more branches where good outcomes are possible, notice them faster, and compound them when they appear. That is almost like increasing the capture cross-section of opportunity.

So pressing luck becomes: expand the frontier without losing recoverability.

Expressing luck becomes: convert favorable contingency into structure, action, or durable advantage.

And the broader ethical point matters too. Once you become good at seeing gradients, asymmetries, incentives, and weak signals, there are two possible directions. One is pure exploitation. The other is building systems that are powerful precisely because they preserve optionality, consent, and reciprocal benefit. The second tends to be more robust because it generates more cooperation and less resistance.

So the version I think is worth keeping is:

see deeply, infer cautiously, act decisively, preserve reversibility where possible, and turn luck into repeatable capability without pretending every success proves the story behind it.

That is strong enough without needing the mythology to carry the factual burden.